Modul Theory
_____________
-> A modul is the smallest independent unit with capacity to relate, and which whose relations define it and give it value.
-> Relations define value, not moduls by themselves.
-> An Object, G, can be defined as the result of n moduls and their links.
   G = {m1 ~ m2 ~ m3 ... mn}, where ~ is the relation operator.
   If G = {a ~ b ~ c ... ~ n}, G = {a ~* n} (composite link) [Note: Objects are often referred to as systems]
-> P ~ Q means P relates to Q, and P(~)Q means relation OF P and Q.
-> -G is the inverse of an object G, such that if G = {a ~ b ~ c ... ~ n}, G = {a ~* n}, then -G = {n ~* a}.
-> Commutative relations are defined as, P <~> Q.
   Not all relations are commutative relations but all commutative relations are relations, of the form.
   P <~> Q <-> (P ~ Q) AND (Q ~ P)

Axioms / Statements:

1. Commutativity of relations:
   P ~ Q and Q ~ P are not necessarily equal: P ~ Q ≠ Q ~ P in general.
   (A relation and its reverse both hold only when P <~> Q.)

2. Potential function p():
   p(P( ~ )Q) = p(P( ~~ )Q), where ~~ is the inverse relation operator.

3. Addition of objects:
   c = a + b
   c~* = ( a~* ) ∪ ( b~* )
   ~*c = ( ~*a ) ∪ ( ~*b )
   (where ∪ is UNION, ~*x means all relations to x, and x~* means all relations of x);

   p(a+b) = p(a) + p(b)
   [Note: this holds when a and b are disjoint (they share no moduls). If they overlap,
   p(a+b) = p(a) + p(b) - p(a ∩ b), where a ∩ b is the part they share.]

4. Uniqueness of objects by relations:
   If ~*a = ~*b AND a~* = b~*, then a = b

5. Composite links:
   If (a ~ b ~ ... ~ n), then (a ~* n)
   p(a( ~* )b) = p(a( ~ )b( ~ ) ... n)

6. Induced subsystems:
   For any subset H ⊆ M, the subsystem on H is:
      G|_H = (H, R ∩ (H × H))
   Relations with at least one endpoint outside H are not part of H.
   Composite links are derived and vanish when any modul in the chain is removed.
   This guarantees locality, composability, and solvability.

7. Multiplication of objects (new axiom):
   Let G = {m1 ~ m2 ~ ... ~ mn} and G' = {m'1 ~ m'2 ~ ... ~ m'k}
   Define the product object G * G' as:

   a) Modules of the product:
      G * G' = { (mi, m'j) | i = 1..n, j = 1..k }
      (Total modules = n * k)

   b) Link formation:
      (mi, m'j) ~ (mr, m's) ⇔ (mi ~ mr in G) AND (m'j ~ m's in G')
      Weight rule: w'((mi,m'j), (mr,m's)) = w(mi,mr) · w(m'j,m's)
      [Note: links form only where both parents are linked, so the product of two objects can be
      disconnected. Joining two objects is done by addition (axiom 3) plus explicit links, not by the product.]

   c) Potential:
      p(G * G') = p(G) * p(G')

   d) Properties:
      - Associative: (G * G') * G'' = G * (G' * G'')
      - Identity element: I = object with 1 module with self-loop, such that G * I = G
      - Inverse compatibility: -(G * G') = (-G) * (-G')

8. Definition of p(), the potential function.

   p(G) = Σ w(a)   (summed over all arcs a in G)

   Components:
   - An arc is one directed relation. A directed relation mi ~ mj is one arc,
     a bidirectional relation mi <~> mj is two arcs (mi ~ mj and mj ~ mi),
     and a self-loop is one arc.
   - w(mi, mj) = Weight of relation between mi and mj
     • For directed: w(mi ~ mj) + w(mj ~ mi) (count both directions)
     • For bidirectional: w(mi <~> mj) (count once in each direction, twice in total)
     • Domain: w ∈ ℝ
     • Default: w = 1 for standard relations
   [Note: counting arcs is what makes p multiplicative. The product of two bidirectional
   relations is two bidirectional relations (four arcs), and 2 * 2 = 4. Counting a bidirectional
   relation once would give 2 ≠ 1 * 1.]

   Properties:
   a) Default: w = 1 for standard relations

   b) Domain: w(mi, mj) ∈ ℝ

   c) Identity: I = {i} with self-loop → p(I) = 1

   d) Zero: Z = ∅ → p(Z) = 0

   e) Isolated modules contribute zero potential

   f) p is a true semiring homomorphism:
      p(a+b) = p(a) + p(b)   (a and b disjoint)
      p(G*G') = p(G) · p(G')

   Interpretation:
   - w > 0: synergistic; w = 0: neutral; w < 0: antagonistic
   - p(G) = total relational weight of system.
   - c₀ (intrinsic capacity) is not used in p; it is used in E (see Stability doc).
